ar so  the crew could easily adapt and change  up the set to look like multiple  different cars we had several train cars  three train cars that they could redress  and swap around but our favorite part  these video walls this was what was so  amazing these they were like 20 feet  tall and football field long okay so  they weren't exactly that big but they  were massive and the video walls were  responsible for playing footage of  rushing scenery outside the train window  they really did bring japan to them  they'd be flashing the trip from tokyo  to kyoto number six  what did you say  this freaky and weirdly cute mascot was  based on an anime character but why was  momomon included in the movie it was to  show some of the wild sights you can see  on a train in japan since there is an  actual pokemon themed car on a real  bullet train cool as it is honestly we  wouldn't want to share a train with this  character it's great  yay number seven yeah this one is brutal  brad you know the consummate  professional he is decided during part  of the choreography to go wouldn't it be  good brad pitt could get a bit caught up  in the moment if i just hit brian in the  nuts over and over again and did maybe  go a bit too far at times  number eight  it's not an action comedy without some  laughs although these ones weren't  always appreciated it's a shock they  made this movie at all knowing how much  joking around happened on set we were  just trying to make each other break and  crack this was partially because david  leach gave us so much room to like  improv and ad-lib so  who was the most fun to make break on  set the best would be getting the camera  men who are like literally trying to  hold the thing together or the boom guy  number nine  this doesn't sound easy  and surprise surprise it wasn't for  david leach the toughest stunt to pull  off visually and technically was when  the guys were flying out of the door  since according to leech we had to have  stuntmen on a wire three riggers and  polar  unfortunately the set posed a problem  since the plan was to kick the guys out  the door but it wouldn't really work out  for that so they got creative number 10  the work never stops being able to adapt  quickly really worked in their favor  director david leach pushed his team  making the stunt team work late and  re-rigging the set just so they could  create a fight and film it quickly how  quickly well leech admitted that  we just did it on the fly and we leaned  in and we got it teamwork always makes  that dream work  number 11.  he knows how to dress  he surprised us with his stellar outfit  choice at the premiere for bullet train  but we had no idea brad pitt was the one  to credit for this wacky yet iconic look  in the movie his director did say  brad came in on fire for big and broad  which is why he opted for the thick  glasses and bucket hat  we don't hate it  number 12  would we expect anything less yeah he  trained a lot while he didn't spend  months preparing he did put in the work  even while the pandemic made it tricky  early on before we were shooting we were  going to his house masking up like  getting our tests and the proof is in  the pudding and getting him in a  fighting shape those workouts worked  number 13.  pitt knew his place he may be a movie  star but he took a hit on set when he  needed to  literally he just was really gracious  about allowing people to slap him  number 14  they were everywhere you looked  since apparently on a train you've got  to be resourceful when it comes to  fighting this team got as creative as  they could when building the fight  scenes but that's not all they seriously  used anything they could on the train as  a weapon chopsticks tables needles  briefcases laptops  number fifteen  she might punch you in the kisser she  spent three films playing a sweet girl  in a kissing booth but joey king brought  all the tough vibes for this role she  even brought the heat doing a lot of her  ow